<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abna al-dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as abnāʾ ad-dawla, the Abnāʾ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The abna al-dawla, Arabic for sons of the regime or dynasty, were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasani</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> who took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a> of 749–750 and their descendants who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. They became the ruling elite of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and formed the mainstay of the caliphal army. The term rarely appears in the sources until the Fourth Fitna of the 810s, when it was applied to the Khurasanis of Baghdad, who overwhelmingly supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>. After al-Ma'mun's victory they were largely replaced by his Persian followers, and under his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> the Turkish slave-soldiers began their rise to power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army that won the revolution of 747–750 came from Khurasan, and its men and their sons, the <b>abna al-dawla</b> (“sons of the dynasty”), settled in Baghdad as the regime’s privileged soldiers, paid by the state.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
